Study 148:                                                                                                              
- Title: Stroke patients’ utilisation of extrinsic feedback from computer-based technology in the home: a multiple case 
study realistic evaluation                                                                                              
- Abstract: BACKGROUND: India is a country with vast unmet medical needs. eHealth has potential to improve quality of   
health care and reach unreached. Sought to understand kinds of eHealth programmes being offered in India today,         
challenges they face, nature of financing. METHODS: Adopted interview-based methodology. 30 interviews represent 28     
organizations, include designers, implementers, evaluators and technology providers for eHealth programmes. RESULTS: A  
range of programmes being run, including point-of-care in rural and urban areas, treatment compliance, data collection  
and disease surveillance, distant medical education. Most programmes provide point-of-care to patients or other         
beneficiaries in rural areas. Technology not limiting factor but unavailability of suitable health personnel major      
challenge, especially in rural areas. Identified few factors that help situation. Financial sustainability also concern 
for most programmes, which have rarely been scaled up. Recent for-profit efforts in urban areas, but no reliable        
business model identified yet. Government facilities not very effective in eHealth on their own, but collaborations     
between government and non-profit (in particular) and for-profit organisations have led to impactful programmes.        
CONCLUSIONS: Unlikely that eHealth will have widespread and sustainable impact without government involvement,          
especially in rural areas. Nevertheless, programmes run solely by government unlikely to be most effective.             
