- Title: Patients’ perspectives of multidisciplinary home-based e-Health service delivery for motor neurone disease     
- Abstract: Purpose: To explore views of people with motor neurone disease (MND) on barriers, facilitators and potential
benefits of using home-based e-Health service delivery (telehealth) to access MND multidisciplinary clinic care.        
Methods: Twelve patients from three MND multidisciplinary clinics and an MND support association group completed survey 
of IT use and participated in interviews, to gather participants’ experiences and perceptions of home-based telehealth  
for MND clinic care. Survey data analyzed descriptively, with interview data analyzed using stepwise inductive approach.
Results: Surveys revealed participants used IT to communicate with family/friends, but less likely to use phone, email  
or videoconferencing with health professionals. Two themes of participants’ use of IT in MND care reflected experiences 
of MND care; and personal preferences for modes of healthcare delivery. Participants willing to use telehealth for MND  
care, with family members acting as patients’ main support for telehealth participation. Nevertheless, participants     
preferred face-to-face contact with MND clinic team in initial and early stages of disease. Conclusions: People living  
with MND may wish to participate in individual care planning to facilitate access to variety of e-Health service        
modalities. Additionally, individual care planning may allow healthcare professionals to deliver e-Health-based care,   
such as telehealth, to increase scope of care provided. Research to ascertain views of health professionals and family  
members as co-participants in service delivery via telehealth needed to fully assess potential contribution of e-Health.
- This is qualitative (descriptive analysis of survey data, stepwise inductive approach for interview data). It explores
