Study 151:                                                                                                              
- Title: A virtual rehabilitation program after amputation: a phenomenological exploration                              
- Abstract: PURPOSE: This study provides an analysis of bodily experiences of a man with a lower leg amputation who used
a virtual rehabilitation program. METHOD: The study reports data from semi-structured interviews with a 32-year veteran 
who used a virtual environment during rehabilitation. Interviews analyzed using interpretative phenomenological analysis
(IPA). RESULTS: During this rehabilitation program, he initially experienced his body as an object, which he had to     
handle carefully. As he went along with training sessions, however, he was more stimulated to react directly without    
being aware of body's position. To allow himself to react spontaneously, needed to gain trust in device. This fostered  
by narrative stressing how device mechanically interacts with movements. CONCLUSION: Use of virtual environment         
facilitated process of re-inserting one's body into flow of one's experience in two opposite, but complementary ways:   
(1) invited to move automatically without taking into account body; (2) invited to take instrumental or rational view on
body. Both processes fostered trust in device, and ultimately in body. IMPLICATIONS FOR REHABILITATION: Providing (more)
technological explanation of technological device (virtual environment) may facilitate rehabilitation process. Providing
(more) explicit technological feedback, during training sessions in virtual environment, may facilitate rehabilitation  
process.                                                                                                                
