### 2. Task outcome (extremely detailed version):
The screening process evaluated 30 studies (indices 124-153) for inclusion in a qualitative evidence synthesis reviewing
factors influencing the provision of home-based rehabilitation and telerehabilitation services. Applying the selection 
criteria requiring qualitative methods exploring stakeholder experiences/perceptions of service provision, 18 studies 
were deemed eligible for inclusion. These studies represented diverse contexts: home-based cardiac rehabilitation 
(studies 124, 140, 145), telerehabilitation via robotics/video conferencing/apps (125, 133, 138, 141, 147, 148, 150, 
151, 152), home-based cognitive training (127), stroke rehabilitation (130, 132, 139), pulmonary rehabilitation (143, 
145), cancer rehabilitation (147), and disability rehabilitation (153). Stakeholders included patients, caregivers, 
healthcare providers, and technology developers. The 12 excluded studies comprised systematic/living reviews (126, 129, 
131, 134, 142, 149), quantitative studies/analyses (129, 142), conceptual papers (136), and policy documents (144, 146),
